<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Drogo of Hauteville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">?–1051</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Drogo of Hauteville was a Norman adventurer who led the Normans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> as count from 1046 until his murder in 1051. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a>, he came to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iron-arm">William Iron Arm</a> and succeeded him, receiving investiture from Emperor Henry III in 1047. He was assassinated in a church at Montolio, probably in a conspiracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> nobles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/drogo-of-hauteville.jpg" alt="Drogo of Hauteville" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giogo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_cath%C3%A9drale_Coutances_Drogon_de_Hauteville.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
